CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure illustrates the Bidirectional Reflectance Distribution Function (BRDF) as a surface interaction model. An incoming light ray strikes a green surface at a point, where the surface normal n points upward and two ray vectors p and q define the local surface coordinate frame. Multiple outgoing arrows emanating in a hemisphere of directions represent the scattered radiance, and the governing equation shows that the reflected intensity depends on the incident radiance, surface orientation, wavelength, and surface material properties. The spread of outgoing rays captures the fact that real surfaces scatter light across many directions rather than reflecting it specularly into a single one. This figure motivates the BRDF as the general mathematical model that describes how a surface converts incoming irradiance into outgoing radiance across all viewing angles.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Large green ground plane drawn as a flat parallelogram (thin slab) with a heavy black outline, filling the lower two-thirds of the frame
  - Small black ellipse on the plane marking the surface hit point (roughly at the horizontal center, slightly left of center, near the front-middle of the plane)
  - Thick incoming light ray ℓ_in entering from the upper-left, drawn as a tapered beam with a yellow-to-orange-to-red gradient along its length, ending in a red arrowhead at the hit point
  - Black unit normal arrow n rising vertically from the hit point
  - Small black right-angle (square corner) marker at the base of n, on the plane, indicating perpendicularity
  - Black in-plane tangent arrow p from the hit point toward the upper-left, lying flat in the surface
  - Black in-plane tangent arrow q from the hit point toward the upper-right, lying flat in the surface
  - Dark green main outgoing arrow ℓ_out leaving the hit point toward the upper-right, longest of the outgoing arrows
  - Hemisphere of ~10 semi-transparent light-green scattered outgoing arrows of varying length radiating from the hit point in all directions — several skimming along the plane toward the viewer/left/right, several rising steeply above it
  - Label ℓ_in(λ) in italic serif near the top-left, alongside the incoming beam
  - Label ℓ_out=F(ℓ_in, n, λ, p, q) in italic serif at the upper-right, at the tip of the main outgoing arrow
  - Bold labels n (above normal arrow), p (left of p arrow), q (right of q arrow)

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World scale: surface plane is a thin slab 10 (X, screen-right) × 0.06 (Y) × 7 (Z, receding), centered at origin on the y=0 plane, top face color #6FE300–#7CFC00 with a black outline of ~0.06 world-unit line width. Hit point at origin (drawn slightly left of the plane's center, in the front half, matching the source), marked by a small black-outlined ellipse of radius ~0.25 × 0.12 lying on the plane. Normal n: black arrow from origin to (0, 1.6, 0), shaft 0.07 radius, cone head 0.18 radius × 0.4 long; bold label 'n' just above the head. Right-angle marker: small black square bracket of side 0.3 in the +X/+Y plane at the base of n, drawn to the lower-right of the shaft as in the source. Tangent p: black arrow from origin toward (-1.15, 0, -0.45) (upper-LEFT, lying flat in the plane), length ~1.25, same head style, bold label 'p' beyond its tip. Tangent q: black arrow from origin toward (+1.15, 0, -0.45) (upper-RIGHT in plane), length ~1.25, bold label 'q' beyond its tip; p and q are perpendicular-ish in-plane basis vectors straddling n symmetrically. Incoming beam ℓ_in: a tapered cylinder/quad-strip from roughly (-3.6, 4.2, 2.0) to the origin — i.e. entering from the upper-LEFT and slightly toward the viewer, at about 50° above the surface — widening from 0.10 to 0.16 radius, vertex-colored gradient yellow #FFE800 at the far end → orange #FF9000 mid → red #FF1A00 at the tip, ending in a red cone arrowhead touching the origin. Label 'ℓ_in(λ)' floats to the right of the beam, about two-thirds up its length. Main outgoing arrow ℓ_out: dark green #1E7B14, from origin toward the mirror direction of ℓ_in (default ≈ (+2.3, 2.6, -1.2)), length ~3.4 (longest arrow); label 'ℓ_out=F(ℓ_in, n, λ, p, q)' at its tip, upper-right. Scattered lobe: ~40–60 arrows from origin sampled over the upper hemisphere (plus a few nearly grazing arrows that skim the plane toward the viewer, left and right, as in the source), color #4CC22E at ~55% opacity, shaft radius 0.04, head 0.11 × 0.25. Arrow length L(ω_o) = 1.0 + 3.0 · f_r(ω_i, ω_o) · cosθ_i, with f_r = k_d/π + k_s·(m+2)/(2π)·max(0, r̂·ω_o)^m, where r̂ = 2(n·ω_i)n − ω_i, m = 2/roughness² − 2; Lambertian sets k_s=0, k_d=1; Glossy k_d=0.35, k_s=0.65; Mirror k_d=0.02, k_s=0.98 with m capped ~4000. cosθ_i = n·ω_i multiplies all lengths so grazing light dims the whole lobe. Palette: green plane #7CFC00, black #000000 for outline/n/p/q/labels, beam yellow→red gradient, lobe greens #1E7B14 (main) and #4CC22E (scattered), white background.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "dragIncoming",
    "type": "drag",
    "teaches": "The whole outgoing radiance lobe is anchored to the incoming direction: swinging ℓ_in around the hemisphere swings the reflection lobe with it (mirrored about n), and at grazing incidence every outgoing arrow shrinks because of the cosθ_i foreshortening of incident irradiance."
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "The scattered arrows form a true 3D hemisphere over the surface point, not a flat fan — the BRDF is defined over all outgoing directions in the upper hemisphere."
  },
  {
    "id": "roughness",
    "type": "slider",
    "label": "Surface roughness",
    "range": [
      0.02,
      1,
      0.01
    ],
    "default": 0.35,
    "teaches": "Roughness controls how sharply energy is concentrated: a smooth surface produces one long arrow near the mirror direction, a rough one spreads comparable-length arrows over the whole hemisphere.",
    "targets": "specular lobe width (Phong exponent ≈ 2/roughness² − 2)"
  },
  {
    "id": "materialModel",
    "type": "toggle",
    "label": "BRDF F",
    "options": [
      "Lambertian",
      "Glossy (diffuse + specular)",
      "Mirror"
    ],
    "default": "Glossy (diffuse + specular)",
    "teaches": "F is the material term in the equation: the same incoming ray produces a uniform hemisphere (Lambertian), a hemisphere with a bright lobe (glossy), or a single spike (mirror) purely by changing F."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Glossy surface, oblique light",
    "narration": "A single incoming ray ℓ_in(λ) strikes the point. Outgoing arrows fill the whole hemisphere, but the longest cluster leans toward the mirror direction — this is ℓ_out = F(ℓ_in, n, λ, p, q).",
    "state": {
      "materialModel": "Glossy (diffuse + specular)",
      "roughness": 0.35
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Perfectly diffuse",
    "narration": "With a Lambertian F, all outgoing arrows have equal length regardless of direction: the surface looks equally bright from every viewpoint, and only the cosine of the incidence angle sets overall brightness.",
    "state": {
      "materialModel": "Lambertian",
      "roughness": 1
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Mirror limit",
    "narration": "Shrink roughness to zero and the hemisphere collapses to one arrow in the mirror direction — specular reflection is just the degenerate case of the BRDF.",
    "state": {
      "materialModel": "Mirror",
      "roughness": 0.02
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Grazing incidence",
    "narration": "Drag ℓ_in toward the horizon: the lobe follows the mirror direction and every arrow shortens, because the same beam now spreads its power over a larger surface patch (cos θ_i falloff).",
    "state": {
      "materialModel": "Glossy (diffuse + specular)",
      "roughness": 0.35
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "rollDeg": 0,
  "heightFraction": 0.6
}

NOTES: Use an orthographic-style axonometric camera (OrthographicCamera or a long-lens PerspectiveCamera) so the plane's opposite edges stay parallel, matching the source's parallelogram. Build a reusable Arrow class from CylinderGeometry + ConeGeometry in a Group, oriented by quaternion from +Y to the target direction, so lengths can be rescaled per frame without rebuilding geometry (use InstancedMesh or a pooled set of ~60 arrow groups for the lobe to keep the recompute at 60fps). The incoming beam's gradient is a vertex-colored tapered tube (TubeGeometry along a straight curve with a radius function) using vertexColors material. Draw the plane outline with a thick Line2/LineSegments2 (fat lines) at ~4px so it reads like the source's heavy black border. Labels (ℓ_in(λ), ℓ_out=F(...), n, p, q) as CSS2DRenderer/HTML overlays with KaTeX or italic serif text so they stay crisp and re-anchor to projected 3D positions each frame — the ℓ_out label must follow the main arrow tip as the lobe swings. Drag interaction: raycast the pointer onto a large invisible sphere of radius 4 centered at the hit point, clamp the result to y > 0.08 so ℓ_in stays above the surface, and update the beam, mirror direction and all lobe lengths in the same frame. Render the semi-transparent scattered arrows with depthWrite:false and a single sorted draw pass so overlapping arrows blend like the flat artwork. Add a soft hemisphere light plus one directional light; keep materials MeshBasic/Lambert-flat to preserve the diagram's poster-flat look rather than a shiny 3D render.

Follow the interaction plan above.

